  "solution": {
    "text": "Since, Penetration of s orbital is the maximum because of the closeness to the nucleus than the p, d and f orbitals<br />&nbsp;it is easier to remove <math><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi></math> electron than&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></math>&nbsp; electron<br /><math> <mrow> <msub> <mtext>Z</mtext> <mrow> <mtext>eff</mtext></mrow> </msub> <mo>&#8733;</mo><mfrac> <mn>1</mn> <mrow> <mtext>shielding&#160;effect</mtext></mrow> </mfrac> </mrow> </math><br /><br /><math> <mrow> <msub> <mrow> <mfenced> <mrow> <msub> <mtext>Z</mtext> <mrow> <mtext>eff</mtext></mrow> </msub> </mrow> </mfenced></mrow> <mtext>B</mtext> </msub> <mo>&#62;</mo><msub> <mrow> <mfenced> <mrow> <msub> <mtext>Z</mtext> <mrow> <mtext>eff</mtext></mrow> </msub> </mrow> </mfenced></mrow> <mrow> <mtext>Be</mtext></mrow> </msub> </mrow> </math> So, shielding effect is more on 2p electron of B than 2s electron of Be<br /><br />So, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mfenced><mtext>Be</mtext></mfenced><mrow><mtext>I</mtext><msub><mtext>.E</mtext><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>&#62;</mo><msub><mfenced><mtext>B</mtext></mfenced><mrow><mtext>I</mtext><msub><mtext>.E</mtext><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub></math>",
